"body": {"content": [{"endIndex": 1, "sectionBreak": {"sectionStyle": {"columnSeparatorStyle": "NONE", "contentDirection": "LEFT_TO_RIGHT", "sectionType": "CONTINUOUS"}}}, {"endIndex": 4069, "paragraph": {"elements": [{"endIndex": 24, "startIndex": 1, "textRun": {"content": "Here's what I found on ", "textStyle": {}}}, {"endIndex": 60, "startIndex": 24, "textRun": {"content": "additional Lancefield antigen groups", "textStyle": {"bold": true}}}, {"endIndex": 357, "startIndex": 60, "textRun": {"content": ": While your current reference manual documents Groups A, B, and D, web research reveals that the Lancefield serological classification system extends through Groups C, E, F, G, H, K, L, M, N, O, R, and S, covering a wide range of human pathogens, commensals, dairy organisms, and animal species. ", "textStyle": {}}}, {"endIndex": 378, "startIndex": 357, "textRun": {"content": "Key themes I noticed:", "textStyle": {"bold": true}}}, {"endIndex": 382, "startIndex": 378, "textRun": {"content": " 1. ", "textStyle": {}}}, {"endIndex": 418, "startIndex": 382, "textRun": {"content": "Clinical Pathogens (Groups C and G):", "textStyle": {"bold": true}}}, {"endIndex": 429, "startIndex": 418, "textRun": {"content": " Primarily ", "textStyle": {}}}, {"endIndex": 455, "startIndex": 429, "textRun": {"content": "Streptococcus dysgalactiae", "textStyle": {"italic": true}}}, {"endIndex": 463, "startIndex": 455, "textRun": {"content": " subsp. ", "textStyle": {}}}, {"endIndex": 474, "startIndex": 463, "textRun": {"content": "equisimilis", "textStyle": {"italic": true}}}, {"endIndex": 486, "startIndex": 474, "textRun": {"content": " (SDSE) and ", "textStyle": {}}}, {"endIndex": 504, "startIndex": 486, "textRun": {"content": "Streptococcus equi", "textStyle": {"italic": true}}}, {"endIndex": 608, "startIndex": 504, "textRun": {"content": ", which cause severe pharyngitis, skin/soft tissue infections, bacteremia, and toxic shock syndrome. 2. ", "textStyle": {}}}, {"endIndex": 651, "startIndex": 608, "textRun": {"content": "Abscess & Deep Tissue Infections (Group F):", "textStyle": {"bold": true}}}, {"endIndex": 672, "startIndex": 651, "textRun": {"content": " Associated with the ", "textStyle": {}}}, {"endIndex": 695, "startIndex": 672, "textRun": {"content": "Streptococcus anginosus", "textStyle": {"italic": true}}}, {"endIndex": 704, "startIndex": 695, "textRun": {"content": " group (\"", "textStyle": {}}}, {"endIndex": 714, "startIndex": 704, "textRun": {"content": "S. milleri", "textStyle": {"italic": true}}}, {"endIndex": 807, "startIndex": 714, "textRun": {"content": "\"), which frequently leads to purulent deep-tissue abscesses and postsurgical infections. 3. ", "textStyle": {}}}, {"endIndex": 858, "startIndex": 807, "textRun": {"content": "Commensal, Dairy & Zoonotic Groups (H, K, N, R, S):", "textStyle": {"bold": true}}}, {"endIndex": 900, "startIndex": 858, "textRun": {"content": " Includes normal oral mucosa species like ", "textStyle": {}}}, {"endIndex": 912, "startIndex": 900, "textRun": {"content": "S. sanguinis", "textStyle": {"italic": true}}}, {"endIndex": 927, "startIndex": 912, "textRun": {"content": " (Group H) and ", "textStyle": {}}}, {"endIndex": 940, "startIndex": 927, "textRun": {"content": "S. salivarius", "textStyle": {"italic": true}}}, {"endIndex": 969, "startIndex": 940, "textRun": {"content": " (Group K), dairy-fermenting ", "textStyle": {}}}, {"endIndex": 987, "startIndex": 969, "textRun": {"content": "Lactococcus lactis", "textStyle": {"italic": true}}}, {"endIndex": 1024, "startIndex": 987, "textRun": {"content": " (Group N), and swine pathogens like ", "textStyle": {}}}, {"endIndex": 1031, "startIndex": 1024, "textRun": {"content": "S. suis", "textStyle": {"italic": true}}}, {"endIndex": 1049, "startIndex": 1031, "textRun": {"content": " (Groups R/S). 4. ", "textStyle": {}}}, {"endIndex": 1082, "startIndex": 1049, "textRun": {"content": "Standard Latex Diagnostic Panels:", "textStyle": {"bold": true}}}, {"endIndex": 4069, "startIndex": 1082, "textRun": {"content": " Routine clinical agglutination test kits (e.g., Streptex, PathoDx) standardly screen for Lancefield Groups A, B, C, D, F, and G together. 📥 The full research report is included as the first source in the import card below — import it to chat with the findings directly. [ {\"version\": \"v0.9\", \"createSurface\": {\"surfaceId\": \"source-import-lancefield-antigens\", \"catalogId\": \"notebooklm:nlm_v1\"}}, {\"version\": \"v0.9\", \"updateComponents\": { \"surfaceId\": \"source-import-lancefield-antigens\", \"components\": [{ \"id\": \"root\", \"component\": \"SourceImportCard\", \"description\": \"Medical articles and microbiology references on additional Lancefield antigen groups\", \"sources\": [ { \"id\": \"src-report\", \"title\": \"Research report: Additional Lancefield Antigen Groups\", \"url\": \"https://contribution.usercontent.google.com/download?c=Cgpub3RlYm9va2xtEkASCWFydGlmYWN0cxozCiQ4ODhhY2IwNS1lZTE0LTQwMzgtOTVhMC0wNTRiMmQ0NjBhYTESCxIHEPrAh_q0DhgB&filename=Research_report_Additional_Lancefield_Antigen_Groups.md&opi=96797242\", \"sourceType\": \"text\", \"sourceName\": \"Research Report\", \"summary\": \"Full research report with 6 sources on additional Lancefield antigen groups\" }, { \"id\": \"src-1\", \"title\": \"Lancefield grouping\", \"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lancefield_grouping\", \"sourceName\": \"Wikipedia\", \"summary\": \"Overview of Lancefield classification covering Groups A through R/S and non-Lancefield streptococci.\" }, { \"id\": \"src-2\", \"title\": \"Pathogenicity Factors in Group C and G Streptococci\", \"url\": \"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11026075/\", \"sourceName\": \"PMC (NIH)\", \"summary\": \"Detailed scientific analysis of Group C and G species, host reservoirs, and disease mechanisms.\" }, { \"id\": \"src-3\", \"title\": \"Lancefield Grouping of Clinically Relevant Beta-Hemolytic Streptococci\", \"url\": \"https://microbeonline.com/lancefield-grouping-clinically-relevant-beta-hemolytic-streptococci/\", \"sourceName\": \"Microbe Online\", \"summary\": \"Summary of clinically relevant beta-hemolytic groups (A, B, C, D, F, G) and diagnostic latex assays.\" }, { \"id\": \"src-4\", \"title\": \"Lancefield-classification of streptococcus\", \"url\": \"https://www.altmeyers.org/en/microbiology/lancefield-classification-of-streptococcus-154246\", \"sourceName\": \"Altmeyers Encyclopedia\", \"summary\": \"Microbiological encyclopedia entry categorizing streptococcal species across Groups A through N, O, R, and S.\" }, { \"id\": \"src-5\", \"title\": \"Pastorex Strep Latex Agglutination Kit Manual\", \"url\": \"https://commerce.bio-rad.com/webroot/web/pdf/inserts/CDG/en/61721_880813_EN.pdf\", \"sourceName\": \"Bio-Rad\", \"summary\": \"Clinical diagnostic manual detailing latex agglutination grouping for Groups A, B, C, D, F, and G.\" }, { \"id\": \"src-6\", \"title\": \"PathoDx Strep Grouping Instructions\", \"url\": \"https://assets.fishersci.com/TFS-Assets/MBD/Instructions/X7773A.pdf\", \"sourceName\": \"Thermo Fisher Scientific\", \"summary\": \"Technical specifications for rapid serological identification of Groups A, B, C, F, and G.\" } ] }] }} ]\n", "textStyle": {}}}], "paragraphStyle": {"direction": "LEFT_TO_RIGHT", "namedStyleType": "NORMAL_TEXT"}}, "startIndex": 1}, {"endIndex": 4070, "paragraph": {"elements": [{"endIndex": 4070, "startIndex": 4069, "textRun": {"content": "\n", "textStyle": {}}}], "paragraphStyle": {"direction": "LEFT_TO_RIGHT", "namedStyleType": "NORMAL_TEXT"}}, "startIndex": 4069}]},
